{
    "LayoutEditor__ModelLayers__Meta": {
        "Meta__FileName"        : "Na__LayoutEditor__ModelLayers__Config__.json",
        "Meta__Description"     : "Maps Noble Architecture SketchUp tag names (the SSOT) to the runtime model category a loaded GLB lands in, to the short label the Model Layers panel shows, and to the edge style that category's projected linework starts with. One row per thing a person would want to switch off, or draw differently, in a drawing.",
        "Meta__Version"         : "1.1.1",
        "Meta__Created"         : "12-Sep-2026",
        "Meta__Author"          : "Adam Noble - Noble Architecture",

        "Meta__SsotSource"      : "Na__DataLib__CoreIndex__Tags__.json",
        "Meta__SsotPath"        : "%AppData%/SketchUp/SketchUp 2026/SketchUp/Plugins/Na__Common__DataLib__CoreSuEntityStandards/",
        "Meta__SsotVersionRead" : "2.3.2",

        "Meta__HowThisIsUsed"   : "The panel lists the categories the model ACTUALLY loaded, not this file: a category here with no GLB behind it never appears, and a category the model has that this file has not heard of still appears under an auto-generated label. This file supplies the wording, the grouping and the edge defaults, never the inventory.",
        "Meta__WhyKeyAndTag"    : "Layer__CategoryKey is the runtime name - the THREE.Group the multi-model loader creates, and what the render, the linework exclusion and the per-segment owner tag all actually match on. Layer__SketchUpTags are the SSOT tag names an object carries in SketchUp, kept here so the two vocabularies stay traceable to each other when either side is renamed.",
        "Meta__WhyNoDefaultOff" : "Every category starts on. A viewport stores only what has been switched OFF, so a model that gains a category later shows it rather than silently hiding it.",

        "Meta__EdgeFields"      : "Layer__EdgeWeightFactor, Layer__EdgeColour and Layer__EdgeLineType are the 2D PROJECTED LINEWORK style this category starts with in every newly drawn viewport. They apply to that layer and nothing else - not the 3D view, not the backing raster, not the markup. The colour and line type aliases are defined in Na__LayoutEditor__EdgeStyles__Config__.json.",
        "Meta__WeightMeaning"   : "The weight is a FACTOR on the sheet's master viewport lineweight, never a width: 1.0 is the master exactly, 0.5 is half of it. Raising the master therefore thickens the whole drawing and leaves the hierarchy - walls over openings over fixtures over furniture - intact.",
        "Meta__DepthCueIntent"  : "The greys are a depth cue, not decoration. Structure is black and full weight because it is what the drawing is about; openings and fixtures step back to dark grey; furniture and planting sit at mid and light grey because they are there for scale and should not compete with the thing being built.",
        "Meta__ExistingIsEqual" : "The Existing group deliberately carries the same weights and colours as Proposed (agreed 12-Sep-2026). Showing retained work lighter is a common convention but it is a drawing decision, not a data one; make it here, per row, when a job wants it.",
        "Meta__Annotation"      : "The Annotation group is the SketchUp LINETYPE tags - the tags a person draws on to say a line is dashed, dotted, a centre line, a clearance, an overhead extent or something coming out. Each one arrives as its own linework-only GLB written by the GlbBuilder's Linetype Linework Export (Glb__LineworkOnly in the Tags SSOT), so the category exists in a drawing only when the model actually holds lines on that tag. Their line types here mirror Glb__LineworkLineType in the SSOT: change one, change the other.",
        "Meta__AnnotationDraws" : "Annotation linework is drawn as authored, never cut at the drawing's cut plane and never occlusion-clipped - a line tagged as overhead is meant to show through the thing above it. Switch a row off in the Model Layers panel to take it off a drawing.",
        "Meta__PerViewport"     : "These are DEFAULTS. A viewport that has had a category restyled stores only that category, in full, in Viewport__ProjectedEdges - so a change to this file moves every viewport that never disagreed."
    },

